  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/colorado-river/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Negotiators relied on only about 20 years of gauge records; a 1976 tree-ring reconstruction put the 1564–1961 average at about 13.5 million acre-feet a year and found 1905–1930 the wettest such period in 400 years, while the Compact and the 1944 treaty together assigned up to 17.5 million.",
   "uso:myth": "The 1922 Colorado River Compact divided a river whose long-term average flow was well known.",
   "uso:why": "The Compact's fixed numbers look like measurements, but they rest on an unusually wet stretch of record, which is a root of today's shortages."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/colorado-river/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the 1922 Compact divide the river at Lee Ferry rather than state by state, and what did it promise each side?",
   "uso:answer": "Herbert Hoover proposed splitting the basin into an Upper and a Lower half; the Compact gave each 7.5 million acre-feet a year (the Lower Basin could add 1 million more) and required the Upper Division not to deplete the flow at Lee Ferry below 75 million acre-feet over any ten years. The Lower Basin's share was divided among Arizona, California and Nevada later, by the 1928 Boulder Canyon Project Act.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:colorado-river"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/colorado-river/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How do tree-ring studies help explain the Colorado River's modern water shortages?",
   "uso:answer": "They show the Compact was based on an unusually wet period (1905–1930), so the long-term flow (about 13.5 million acre-feet a year in one reconstruction) is far below the 16.5 million acre-feet promised to the two basins plus 1.5 million to Mexico.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:colorado-river"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/colorado-river/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How were Native nations' water rights treated in the Compact and later recognized?",
   "uso:answer": "No tribe took part in the 1922 negotiations, and the Compact only said it did not affect U.S. obligations to tribes; in Arizona v. California (1963) the Supreme Court applied the 1908 Winters doctrine and held that five lower-river reservations had reserved enough water to irrigate their practicably irrigable land.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:colorado-river"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/colorado-river/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Hopi guides lead a party under García López de Cárdenas, part of Coronado's expedition, to the rim of the Grand Canyon.",
