   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Troops stayed all year while the Nine faced daily harassment and one of them was expelled; in September 1958 Faubus closed all four Little Rock high schools under a new state law, voters backed closure three to one, and the schools did not reopen until August 1959.",
   "uso:myth": "Once federal troops escorted the Little Rock Nine into Central High in September 1957, the school and the city's schools were desegregated and the crisis was over.",
   "uso:why": "The famous images of paratroopers escorting the students compress a multi-year struggle into one moment of victory and hide how long and how deliberately segregationists resisted after the troops arrived."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did President Eisenhower send federal troops to Little Rock even though he was reluctant to act as a civil rights champion?",
   "uso:answer": "He framed the deployment as enforcing the orders of federal courts against mob obstruction, arguing that if the executive did not uphold court rulings the rule of law would collapse; he also worried that the crisis was damaging America's standing abroad during the Cold War.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Little Rock crisis lead to Cooper v. Aaron, and what did the Court decide?",
   "uso:answer": "After a year of turmoil the school board sought to suspend its desegregation plan; in September 1958 the Supreme Court unanimously refused, holding under the Supremacy Clause that state officials cannot nullify federal court orders based on Brown or war against the Constitution they swore to support.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians disagree about Governor Orval Faubus's motives?",
   "uso:answer": "Before 1957 he was seen as a racial moderate who had hired Black state employees and supported Black colleges, so his biographer argues his stand was a political calculation to win re-election in 1958, while some observers insisted catering to segregationists was out of character for him; many voters rewarded him for appearing to stand up to the federal government.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court rules in Brown v. Board of Education that segregated public schools are unconstitutional.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1954-05-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Escorted by federal troops, the Little Rock Nine attend their first full day of classes.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The 101st Airborne returns to Fort Campbell, leaving federalized Guard units in charge for the rest of the year.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1957-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Minnijean Brown is expelled after retaliating against harassment.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1958-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ernest Green becomes the first Black student to graduate from Central High.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1958-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Little Rock School Board adopts Superintendent Virgil Blossom's gradual plan, beginning with token desegregation of Central High in 1957.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1955-05-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Federal judge Ronald Davies voids a state court injunction against integration and orders the district to proceed.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-08-28"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Governor Orval Faubus announces on television that he has called out the Arkansas National Guard.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Guardsmen turn the Black students away; fifteen-year-old Elizabeth Eckford walks alone past a jeering crowd.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Faubus and Eisenhower meet at Newport, Rhode Island, without reaching agreement.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Judge Davies orders Faubus and the Guard to stop interfering, and Faubus withdraws the troops.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The students enter Central, but a mob of more than 1,000 forms outside and they are taken out by lunchtime; Eisenhower issues Proclamation 3204.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Eisenhower signs Executive Order 10730, federalizing the Arkansas Guard and sending in the 101st Airborne Division.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The clauses protect against any government deprivation of life, liberty, or property, including civil and administrative actions such as cutting off government benefits (Goldberg v. Kelly, 1970; Mathews v. Eldridge, 1976), and courts weigh how much process each situation requires.",
   "uso:myth": "Due process is only about criminal trials.",
   "uso:why": "The most familiar procedural rights, such as indictment, jury trial and counsel, sit beside due process in the Fifth and Sixth Amendments, so people assume the clause belongs only to the courtroom."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why does the Constitution contain two Due Process Clauses?",
   "uso:answer": "The Fifth Amendment's clause was held in Barron v. Baltimore (1833) to bind only the federal government, so after the Civil War the Fourteenth Amendment (ratified 1868) added a parallel clause forbidding the states to deprive any person of life, liberty, or property without due process of law.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does a court decide how much process is \"due\" when the government ends someone's benefits?",
   "uso:answer": "Under Mathews v. Eldridge (1976) it balances three factors, the private interest affected, the risk of an erroneous deprivation under the procedures used, and the government's interest, against a baseline of notice and an opportunity to be heard.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is substantive due process more controversial than procedural due process or incorporation?",
   "uso:answer": "Critics argue the clause's text guarantees lawful procedure, not limits on what laws may say, and note the doctrine's roots in Dred Scott and Lochner; defenders answer that more than a century of precedent and the Ninth Amendment support protecting unenumerated rights, and that the real question is how judges should identify them.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Magna Carta promises that free men will be judged by their peers or by the law of the land.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1215"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment extends a due process guarantee against the states.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-07-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Lochner v. New York strikes down a workplace regulation on \"freedom of contract\" grounds.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1905"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "West Coast Hotel Co. v. Parrish repudiates the Lochner approach to economic regulation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1937"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Mullane v. Central Hanover Bank sets minimum requirements of notice, a hearing and an impartial tribunal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1950"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Mathews v. Eldridge adopts a three-factor balancing test for the process due in civil proceedings.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1976"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An English statute restating Magna Carta first uses the phrase \"due process of law.\"",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1354"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Sir Edward Coke's Second Institutes equates \"law of the land\" with \"due process of law,\" shaping the founders' understanding.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1641"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Maryland's and Virginia's new constitutions adopt Magna Carta's \"law of the land\" protections.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1776"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress proposes twelve amendments, including the article containing the Due Process Clause.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1789-09-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Bill of Rights adds the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause to the Constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1791-12-15"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "New York becomes the first state to write \"due process of law\" into its own constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1821"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Barron v. Baltimore holds that the Fifth Amendment and the rest of the Bill of Rights limit only the federal government.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1833"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Dred Scott the Court suggests that freeing an enslaved man would deprive his owner of property without due process.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1857"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Dust Bowl refugees were only part of a migration of roughly half a million people from several Southern Plains states; many came from Arkansas, Missouri, eastern Oklahoma and East Texas, where tenant farmers were pushed off the land by falling prices, foreclosures and landlords using federal crop payments to mechanize and evict them.",
   "uso:myth": "The \"Okies\" who poured into California in the 1930s were mostly farm families blown out of the Dust Bowl.",
   "uso:why": "John Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath and Depression-era photographs fused the dust storms and the westward migration into one story, and Californians labelled all the newcomers \"Okies\"."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did drought in the 1930s produce such severe dust storms on the Southern Plains?",
   "uso:answer": "Between about 1909 and 1929 farmers had broken some 32 million acres of native sod, much of it for wheat; when drought killed the crops from 1931, bare plowed fields lost the grass roots that had held the soil, and high winds lifted it away.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Dust Bowl change federal policy?",
   "uso:answer": "Dust clouds reaching Washington in 1934 and 1935 helped Hugh Hammond Bennett persuade Congress to pass the Soil Conservation Act of 1935, which created the permanent Soil Conservation Service; it promoted terracing, strip cropping and grass seeding and led to locally run soil conservation districts.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians disagree about the causes of the Dust Bowl?",
   "uso:answer": "Donald Worster argued that a market-driven plow-up of marginal grassland made it a human-made ecological disaster, while later researchers using county data and climate models stress that dust storms are natural in long Plains droughts, that the heaviest wheat areas were not always the worst hit, and that ocean temperatures drove the drought.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Were most migrants who went to California from the Dust Bowl itself?",
   "uso:answer": "No. Of roughly half a million Southern Plains migrants, many were tenant farmers from Arkansas, Missouri, eastern Oklahoma and East Texas displaced by economic forces rather than drought.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Over the next two decades farmers break about 32 million acres of Great Plains sod, much of it for wheat.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1909"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first soil conservation district is organized, in North Carolina's Brown Creek watershed.",
