   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "E pluribus unum has been the motto of the Great Seal since 1782 and is a required coin inscription, but no statute designated it the national motto; the 1956 act declaring \"In God we trust\" is the law that names one, and E pluribus unum stayed on the seal and coins.",
   "uso:myth": "E pluribus unum was the official national motto of the United States until 1956, when Congress replaced it with \"In God We Trust.\"",
   "uso:why": "The phrase appeared on the seal, coins and the dollar bill for so long that it was widely treated as the country's motto, and even an editor's note on the online edition of a 1923 scholarly study says it was replaced in 1956."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why does the Great Seal's motto describe \"many\" becoming \"one\", and what did the \"many\" originally mean?",
   "uso:answer": "In 1776 and 1782 the motto referred to the thirteen states joining in one union; Du Simitière's design ringed the shield with thirteen escutcheons for the states, and the official 1782 remarks say the motto alludes to the union of the states under Congress.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did a motto from a London magazine end up on the seal, and why is it uncertain who chose it?",
   "uso:answer": "The phrase was printed on the title page of the widely read Gentleman's Magazine, which educated colonists knew. It first appears in the seal record in Du Simitière's August 1776 design for the Franklin-Adams-Jefferson committee, but no document says who suggested it, so the State Department credits Du Simitière, Deutsch argued for Franklin, and the Jefferson Papers editors leave it open.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is it inaccurate to say \"In God We Trust\" replaced E pluribus unum as the national motto in 1956?",
   "uso:answer": "E pluribus unum had never been designated the national motto by law. The 1956 act was the first to declare a national motto, and E pluribus unum remained on the Great Seal and is still required by law on the reverse of every U.S. coin.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Pierre Motteux's London Gentleman's Journal carries the motto, the earliest printing Deutsch traced; the Gentleman's Magazine later puts it on its title page.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1692-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Continental Congress appoints Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and Thomas Jefferson as a committee to design a seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-07-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The committee reports a design drawn up with artist Pierre Eugène Du Simitière bearing the motto E PLURIBUS UNUM; Congress tables it.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-08-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A second committee, advised by Francis Hopkinson, adds the 13 stripes, 13 stars and olive branch but produces no accepted seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1780-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress hands the work of all three committees to its secretary, Charles Thomson, who puts the motto on a scroll in the eagle's beak.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Confederation Congress adopts the Great Seal with the motto.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Franklin D. Roosevelt approves a one-dollar bill design showing both sides of the Great Seal, putting the motto on the back of the note.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1935"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An act of Congress declares 'In God we trust' the national motto; E pluribus unum stays on the seal and coins.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1956-07-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress reenacts the national-motto statute without changing its wording.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2002-11-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Governments classify people constantly, by age, income, or occupation, and most such lines stand if they are \"rationally related to a legitimate state interest\"; only suspect classifications such as race, or laws burdening fundamental rights, face the demanding test of strict scrutiny.",
   "uso:myth": "The Equal Protection Clause forbids government from ever treating people differently.",
   "uso:why": "Equal protection requires equal treatment of those who are \"similarly situated,\" not identical treatment of everyone, so the real question a court asks is which differences a law may lawfully draw on."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the framers of the Fourteenth Amendment write an equal protection guarantee into the Constitution rather than rely on ordinary legislation?",
   "uso:answer": "They wanted to give civil rights measures such as the Civil Rights Act of 1866 a constitutional footing that a later simple majority in Congress could not repeal, so that the equal legal standing of formerly enslaved people could not be undone by shifting politics.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:equal-protection"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How can two laws that both classify people be judged so differently under the Equal Protection Clause?",
   "uso:answer": "Courts apply different levels of review depending on the classification. Most laws need only a rational relationship to a legitimate interest, but a law that sorts people by race (a suspect classification) or burdens a fundamental right must survive strict scrutiny, meaning it must be narrowly tailored to a compelling government interest.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:equal-protection"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The House approves the proposed Fourteenth Amendment, 120-32, carrying the equal protection language.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1866-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment writes the Equal Protection Clause into the Constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-07-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Slaughter-House Cases recognize formerly enslaved African Americans as the clause's primary intended beneficiaries while noting its words reach everyone.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1873"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Yick Wo v. Hopkins applies equal protection beyond race to discriminatory enforcement of an ordinance.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1886"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Brown v. Board of Education holds that segregated public schools violate equal protection.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1954"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Reed v. Reed applies heightened review to a sex-based classification for the first time.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1971"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Craig v. Boren sets the intermediate-scrutiny standard for sex classifications.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1976"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Obergefell v. Hodges holds that state bans on same-sex marriage violate the clause.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2015"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The amendment only forbade denying the vote because of race, color or previous servitude; it conferred no right of suffrage on anyone, did not cover sex, so Black women were still excluded, and left states free to impose poll taxes, literacy tests and other qualifications that Southern states used to disfranchise most Black men until federal enforcement under the Voting Rights Act of 1965.",
   "uso:myth": "The Fifteenth Amendment gave all Black Americans the right to vote in 1870.",
   "uso:why": "Treating 1870 as the moment Black Americans won the vote hides nearly a century of disfranchisement and explains poorly why the civil rights movement still had to fight for the ballot in the 1960s."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why could Southern states keep most Black men from voting for decades even though the Fifteenth Amendment was in force?",
   "uso:answer": "The amendment only barred denial of the vote on account of race, color or previous servitude and left states free to set other qualifications, so states used poll taxes, literacy tests, grandfather clauses and discriminatory administration, while 1876 Supreme Court rulings such as United States v. Reese weakened federal enforcement.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fifteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the amendment divide the women's rights movement?",
   "uso:answer": "Because it protected against racial but not sex discrimination, Lucy Stone and Frederick Douglass supported it as a first step while Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Susan B. Anthony opposed any amendment that excluded women; the American Equal Rights Association broke up in 1869 and two rival suffrage organizations formed.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fifteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How is the Voting Rights Act of 1965 connected to the Fifteenth Amendment?",
   "uso:answer": "The Act used the enforcement power in the amendment's Section 2; President Johnson asked Congress for a law that would make it impossible to thwart the amendment, and the Act abolished remaining barriers and authorized federal supervision of registration.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fifteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress enfranchises Black men in the District of Columbia and the territories and requires seceded states to guarantee Black male suffrage as a condition of readmission.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1867"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Lane v. Wilson holds that the amendment reaches subtle as well as overt discrimination.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1939"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President Lyndon B. Johnson asks Congress for a law to make the amendment impossible to evade.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1965-03-15"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Rice v. Cayetano holds that the amendment protects all persons and that ancestry can serve as a proxy for race.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2000"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court strikes down a key federal oversight provision of the Voting Rights Act.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2013"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Representative George Boutwell proposes a suffrage amendment in the House.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1869-01-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "After a conference committee narrows the text to racial discrimination, Congress gives final approval (House 144 to 44, Senate 39 to 13).",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1869-02-26"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The American Equal Rights Association splits over the amendment's omission of women at its annual meeting.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1869-05-12"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fifteenth-amendment/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The amendment is ratified by three-fourths of the states.",
   "uso:date": {
