    "@value": "1935"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The walkout began on the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad and spread largely on its own along the rail lines; almost none of the strikers belonged to a union and no leaders or organization controlled the strike as a whole, although local groups such as a railroad firemen's organization in West Virginia and socialists in Chicago and St. Louis played real roles in particular places.",
   "uso:myth": "The 1877 strike was a planned national uprising, organized and directed by labor unions or radical leaders.",
   "uso:why": "The strike's sudden national reach and the visible part socialists played in a few cities made it look coordinated, and treating it as a conspiracy obscures the wage cuts and hard times that drove ordinary workers and their neighbours into the streets."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did a wage cut on one railroad turn into a crisis that stretched from Baltimore to St. Louis?",
   "uso:answer": "Railroad workers had already suffered years of depression and repeated wage cuts since 1873, so when the Baltimore and Ohio announced another ten percent cut, the walkout at Martinsburg spread along the connected trunk lines, drew in unemployed people and workers in other trades, and local police and militias could not or would not stop it.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the federal government become involved, and what limits did President Hayes set on that role?",
   "uso:answer": "Governors who said they could not keep order formally asked for federal help, and Hayes issued proclamations and sent Army troops; he refused the Pennsylvania Railroad's request to act directly for the companies and did not use soldiers to run trains, describing his aim as restoring order, though the deployment set a precedent for later presidents.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What lessons did workers and employers each draw from the strike?",
   "uso:answer": "Many workers concluded they needed lasting, organized unions, while businesses concluded they needed more political influence and government help, including better-prepared state forces to deal with future labor unrest.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The railroads' financial bubble bursts, beginning years of depression, layoffs and repeated wage cuts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1873"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Railroad workers at Martinsburg, West Virginia, walk off the job over a ten percent wage cut on the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad and stop trains.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-16"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Governor Henry M. Mathews asks for federal help, and President Rutherford B. Hayes proclaims that those obstructing the railroad must disperse by noon the next day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-18"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Federal troops arrive at Martinsburg and end the violence there.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Hayes issues a similar proclamation for Maryland; in Pittsburgh, militia from Philadelphia fire into a crowd near the 28th Street crossing, killing about twenty people.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-21"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Fires in Pittsburgh destroy the Pennsylvania Railroad's Union Depot, more than thirty buildings and thousands of locomotives and cars.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Unrest in Chicago peaks over five days of walkouts, sympathy strikes and clashes with police, militia and U.S. Army troops.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By the end of July the strikes have largely been put down along the trunk lines.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-31"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By early 1880 the railroads have restored the wages they had cut.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1880"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Records and her own accounts show about 13 trips, all to Maryland's Eastern Shore, freeing about 70 people, while she gave directions to about 70 more who escaped on their own. The only documented reward was $100 each for her and two brothers in 1849, offered by slaveholders who did not know she was Tubman.",
   "uso:myth": "Tubman made 19 trips into the South and rescued more than 300 people, with a $40,000 bounty on her head.",
   "uso:why": "The larger numbers come from Sarah Bradford's 1868–69 biography and an 1867 letter supporting her pension claim; they stuck because they suited a legend, but they obscure how carefully she chose people she knew and trusted."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Tubman return only to Maryland's Eastern Shore rather than travel all over the South?",
   "uso:answer": "Her missions were to bring away family members and friends she knew and trusted; she knew that landscape from her years of labor in its marshes and woods, and unfamiliar places were far more dangerous.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 change where Tubman took the people she rescued?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1850 law put any freedom seeker in the United States, even in the North, at risk of recapture, so she guided people beyond Philadelphia to St. Catharines, Ontario, in Canada.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Tubman have to petition Congress for a pension, and what did she get?",
   "uso:answer": "She had served the Union as a nurse, cook, scout and spy without formal enlistment or regular pay, and at first received only an $8-a-month widow's pension as Nelson Davis's widow; in 1899 Congress raised it to $20 a month for her service as a nurse.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Born Araminta \"Minty\" Ross, probably in February or early March, on Anthony Thompson's plantation at Peter's Neck, Dorchester County, Maryland.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1822"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/10",
