  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The marchers had agreed not to carry firearms or clubs, the state's historical marker and a National Park Service study describe them as unarmed, and over a dozen marchers were shot in the back while fleeing; exactly how the first shots came is still debated.",
   "uso:myth": "The \"Lattimer riots\" were a violent uprising by armed foreign miners that deputies had to put down.",
   "uso:why": "Early newspaper accounts came largely from the sheriff and coal-company sympathizers, the trial records have been lost, and nativist hostility toward Slavic immigrants made the \"riot\" label easy to accept, so some newspapers still used it in the 1930s."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why were the Lattimer marchers on strike, and why were immigrant miners especially vulnerable in 1897?",
   "uso:answer": "They were protesting low wages, high prices for supplies the company sold them and abuses by company-town staff. As recent Polish, Slovak and Lithuanian immigrants, none of them citizens, they faced nativist hostility, a new state tax on employers of non-citizens, and a sheriff's posse drawn from Anglo-American townspeople and company agents.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:lattimer-massacre"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did a massacre that ended in acquittals end up strengthening the miners' union?",
   "uso:answer": "The killings united the region's immigrant communities and disproved the idea that Slavic and Italian workers were docile; within four months more than 15,000 anthracite workers joined the United Mine Workers, which under John Mitchell led the 1900 and 1902 strikes that brought presidential intervention.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:lattimer-massacre"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Lattimer massacre fade from national memory, and how was it revived?",
   "uso:answer": "Scholars point to the victims' ethnicity, nativist attitudes, the site's rural remoteness and coal operators' interest in forgetting. Interest revived in the civil rights era, leading to a 1972 memorial dedicated with Cesar Chavez, centennial markers in 1997 and an archaeological survey in 2010.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:lattimer-massacre"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Pennsylvania enacts the Campbell Act, a tax of three cents a day on coal operators for each non-citizen they employ.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1897-08-21"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Sheriff James Martin's deputies fire on about 400 marching strikers near Lattimer, killing nineteen and wounding dozens.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1897-09-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Martin and his deputies go on trial for murder at the Luzerne County Courthouse in Wilkes-Barre.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1898-02-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/lattimer-massacre/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The jury acquits all the defendants.",
   "uso:date": {
