   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "It was a separate statement made more than eighty years later, in Theodore Roosevelt's December 1904 message to Congress. The 1823 doctrine asked European powers to stay out of the hemisphere; the corollary claimed a right for the United States itself to intervene.",
   "uso:myth": "The Roosevelt Corollary was simply part of the Monroe Doctrine as announced in 1823.",
   "uso:why": "Roosevelt presented his policy as an extension of Monroe's, and the two are usually taught together, so the later claim of a U.S. police power is easily read back into 1823."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did a crisis over Venezuela's debts prompt Roosevelt to rethink the Monroe Doctrine?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1902 European blockade of Venezuela showed that European powers would use force to collect debts in the Americas; Roosevelt feared such actions could become invasions, so he claimed a U.S. right to step in first and make sure creditors were paid.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:roosevelt-corollary"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the corollary turn the Monroe Doctrine's meaning around?",
   "uso:answer": "Monroe's 1823 doctrine was a largely passive warning to Europeans not to intervene in the hemisphere, while the corollary justified U.S. intervention, including later occupations of Haiti and the Dominican Republic.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:roosevelt-corollary"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Dominican customs receivership show the corollary in practice?",
   "uso:answer": "A U.S.-appointed receiver collected Dominican customs duties and applied them to receivership costs and a refinanced debt before passing the rest to the Dominican government, which could not raise its debt or change tariffs without U.S. agreement.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:roosevelt-corollary"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Roosevelt use executive agreements in the Dominican case?",
   "uso:answer": "Critics said his January 1905 agreement bypassed the Senate, and when Democratic opposition blocked a treaty, he put the receivership into effect by a modus vivendi until the Senate approved a convention in 1907.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:roosevelt-corollary"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "James Monroe's annual message warns European powers against new colonization or intervention in the Americas.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1823-12-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "At the Montevideo conference the United States pledges non-intervention in the hemisphere.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1933"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/roosevelt-corollary/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Franklin D. Roosevelt's Good Neighbor policy officially abandons the corollary.",
   "uso:date": {
