  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Article III creates \"one supreme Court\" but sets no size; the Judiciary Act of 1789 gave it six justices, Congress changed the authorized number several times, and the figure of nine dates only from an 1869 statute.",
   "uso:myth": "The Constitution requires the Supreme Court to have nine justices.",
   "uso:why": "Because nine has stood for more than 150 years, an ordinary act of Congress is easily mistaken for a constitutional rule."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why can Congress change the number of Supreme Court justices without amending the Constitution?",
   "uso:answer": "Article III creates \"one supreme Court\" but says nothing about its size, so the number is set by statute; the Judiciary Act of 1789 set six, and later laws moved it between five (never in effect) and ten before an 1869 act fixed it at nine.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Marbury v. Madison expand the Court's role in the system of checks and balances?",
   "uso:answer": "Chief Justice John Marshall held that part of the Judiciary Act of 1789 conflicted with the Constitution and that courts must follow the Constitution when a statute conflicts with it, the first time the Court declared an act of Congress unconstitutional and the basis of judicial review.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Franklin D. Roosevelt's 1937 plan to enlarge the Court fail?",
   "uso:answer": "Many saw it as a threat to judicial independence; a bipartisan Senate Judiciary Committee majority urged its emphatic rejection, the Court began upholding New Deal-era laws such as the minimum-wage law in West Coast Hotel v. Parrish, Justice Van Devanter retired, and the Senate defeated the plan on July 22, 1937.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does most of the Court's work reach it, and how selective is it?",
   "uso:answer": "Most cases arrive as petitions for a writ of certiorari asking the Court to review a lower-court decision; four justices must agree to take a case, and of up to about ten thousand requests a year the Court accepts fewer than 2 percent.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Judiciary Act of 1789 organizes the Court with a chief justice and five associate justices.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1789-09-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress reduces the authorized seats to seven, to be reached as vacancies occur.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1866-07-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress fixes the Court at nine justices, where it has remained.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1869-04-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Chief Justice Charles Evans Hughes lays the cornerstone of the Supreme Court Building.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1932-10-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court moves into its own building in its 146th year.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1935"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President Franklin D. Roosevelt asks Congress for up to six additional justices.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1937-02-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/15",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court upholds a Washington State minimum-wage law for women in West Coast Hotel v. Parrish.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1937-03-29"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/16",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Roosevelt's plan to enlarge the Court is defeated in the Senate.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1937-07-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/17",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Thurgood Marshall is sworn in as the first African American justice.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1967-10-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/18",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Sandra Day O'Connor is sworn in as the first woman justice.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1981-09-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court first convenes in the Exchange Building in New York City, a day late because some justices could not arrive in time.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1790-02-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court issues its first opinion, in West v. Barnes.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1791-08-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court follows the capital from Philadelphia to Washington and is lent space in the Capitol.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1800"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Marbury v. Madison the Court for the first time declares an act of Congress unconstitutional.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1803"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress adds a seventh seat.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1807-02-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress adds two more seats, bringing the Court to nine.",
