  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The treaty was made between Britain and the United States alone, signed only by their commissioners, and contained no terms at all for Britain's Native allies; Britain signed away only its own claims, and each nation was left to make its own peace with the United States, which at Fort Stanwix in 1784 treated four of the Six Nations as defeated enemies. A Native confederacy north of the Ohio went on to defeat U.S. armies in 1790 and 1791.",
   "uso:myth": "In the Treaty of Paris, the Native nations east of the Mississippi gave up their lands to the United States.",
   "uso:why": "The treaty's boundary lines put the whole region between the Appalachians and the Mississippi inside the United States on paper, which makes it easy to read a European diplomatic agreement as if it settled who actually held and governed the land."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the American commissioners sign preliminary terms with Britain in November 1782 that could not take effect at once?",
   "uso:answer": "The United States was allied with France, so the preliminary articles were written to take effect only after Britain and France agreed their own peace terms; France and Britain signed their preliminaries on January 20, 1783, and the definitive treaty followed on September 3.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Article 7 of the treaty, which barred the British from carrying away enslaved people, end up being applied in New York?",
   "uso:answer": "Sir Guy Carleton read it as not covering Black people who had already joined the British, so they could not be returned as property; about 3,000 in New York received passes recorded in the Book of Negroes, and upwards of 9,000 people of African descent left with the British from New York and other ports.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the treaty leave the Haudenosaunee and other Native nations to negotiate separately with the United States?",
   "uso:answer": "The treaty was between Britain and the United States only and contained no negotiated terms for Britain's Native allies, so each nation had to make its own peace, as the Six Nations did under pressure at Fort Stanwix in 1784.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did disputes over the treaty's debt and western-post terms help make the case for a stronger national government?",
   "uso:answer": "Virginia closed its courts to British creditors because Britain had broken the treaty, and British garrisons stayed at Detroit, Niagara and other posts inside U.S. borders; Federalists argued that only a Congress able to tax and raise an army could force Britain to leave.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Formal peace negotiations open in Paris between British envoys and the American commissioners Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and John Jay.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-09-27"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The British and American commissioners sign preliminary articles of peace, to take effect only once Britain and France agree terms.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-11-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Britain and France sign their own preliminary articles, and an armistice is declared.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1783-01-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "David Hartley and the three American commissioners sign the definitive treaty at the Hôtel de York in Paris; Britain's treaties with France and Spain are signed at Versailles the same day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1783-09-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Confederation Congress, meeting in Annapolis, ratifies the treaty.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1784-01-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "With no terms for Britain's Native allies in the treaty, U.S. commissioners dictate a separate peace to the Six Nations at Fort Stanwix.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1784-10-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Federal law treats tribal self-government as inherent, held before the United States existed and acknowledged in treaties and the Constitution rather than created by them; tribal status is political, attaching to nations and their citizens, not to race.",
   "uso:myth": "Tribal sovereignty is a privilege the federal government granted to Native Americans as a racial group.",
   "uso:why": "Because Congress can limit tribal powers and federal agencies administer many tribal programs, sovereignty is easy to mistake for a federal gift, and because tribal citizens are Native people, a political status is easy to mistake for a racial one."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Supreme Court in Worcester v. Georgia hold that Georgia's laws could not reach the Cherokee Nation?",
   "uso:answer": "Chief Justice John Marshall reasoned that Indian nations had always been distinct, independent political communities whose relations with the United States ran through treaties and federal law, so the Cherokee Nation was a distinct community occupying its own territory where state law had no force, and the Georgia statute used to convict Samuel Worcester was void.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:tribal-sovereignty"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How is tribal sovereignty both inherent and limited under federal law?",
   "uso:answer": "The Court treats tribal powers as inherent, existing before and apart from the United States, yet it has also held that Congress has broad (plenary but not absolute) power over Indian affairs and can restrict tribal authority, as when courts held tribes lack criminal jurisdiction over non-Indians without congressional authorization.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:tribal-sovereignty"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did termination policy in the 1950s and 1960s run against the principle of tribal sovereignty?",
   "uso:answer": "Termination sought to end federal recognition and trust protection of tribes and shifted reservation jurisdiction to states; Congress began 60 termination proceedings and more than three million acres of tribal land were lost before the policy was repudiated in 1970 in favor of self-determination.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:tribal-sovereignty"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The United States signs its first treaty with a Native nation, the Lenape (Delaware); both sides treat early treaties as diplomatic agreements between sovereigns.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1778"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/tribal-sovereignty/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "California v. Cabazon Band of Mission Indians holds that state regulation of on-reservation bingo would infringe tribal government.",
   "uso:date": {
