    "@value": "1927-03-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Its first classes met in Montgomery, Alabama, in March–May 1910, on land that later became Maxwell Air Force Base; only then did the school settle at Huffman Prairie near Dayton.",
   "uso:myth": "The Wright brothers' flying school began in Dayton, Ohio.",
   "uso:why": "The school is remembered through Dayton and its aviation park, while the short, troubled Alabama camp produced a single graduate and is mostly recalled locally."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Wrights start their school in Montgomery rather than in Dayton, and why did it move back so soon?",
   "uso:answer": "Dayton's winter weather was poor for flying, and Montgomery offered less rain and suitable winds; but engine trouble, weather and the need to prepare for exhibition tours in June led Wilbur to close the camp in May 1910 and move training to Huffman Field.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:wright-company-school-of-aviation"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the school help shape American military aviation?",
   "uso:answer": "Army officers such as Henry H. Arnold and Thomas Milling learned to fly there in 1911 and then helped start the Army's first permanent flight school at College Park, Maryland; Arnold later commanded the Army Air Corps.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:wright-company-school-of-aviation"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the school serve the Wright Company's business as well as teaching?",
   "uso:answer": "It shared a hangar with the company's test flying and exhibition team, produced pilots for the exhibition circuit, and marketed the Wrights' airplanes to civilian and military buyers.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:wright-company-school-of-aviation"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Wilbur Wright picks Frank Kohn's plantation near Montgomery, Alabama, for spring flight training.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1910-02-16"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Orville makes Montgomery's first powered flight; training of the first students follows.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1910-03-26"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/wright-company-school-of-aviation/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The school moves its permanent operations to Huffman Field (Simms Station) east of Dayton.",
   "uso:date": {
